"www.fftw.org (https://www.fftw.org/~athena/abstracts/abstract12.html)\nciteturn11view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.fftw.org/~athena/abstracts/abstract12.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 21\nL0: Abstract\nL1: \nL2: * * *\nL3: \nL4: ### Cache-Oblivious Algorithms\nL5: \nL6: by 【0†Matteo Frigo】, 【1†Charles E. Leiserson†supertech.lcs.mit.edu】, 【2†Harald Prokop†supertech.lcs.mit.edu】, and Sridhar Ramachandran.\nL7: \nL8: In the 40th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science, FOCS '99, 17-18 October, 1999, New York, NY, USA.\nL9: 2019 IEEE FOCS Test of Time Award (for 1999)\nL10: 【3†[Full text]】\nL11: \nL12: * * *\nL13: ### Abstract\nL14: This paper presents asymptotically optimal algorithms for rectangular matrix transpose, FFT, and sorting on computers with multiple levels of caching. Unlike previous optimal algorithms, these algorithms are cache oblivious: no variables dependent on hardware parameters, such as cache size and cache-line length, need to be tuned to achieve optimality. Nevertheless, these algorithms use an optimal amount of work and move data optimally among multiple levels of cache.\nL15: For a cache with size Z and cache-line length L where Z=\\Omega(L^2) the number of cache misses for an m\\times n matrix transpose is \\Theta(1+mn/L). The number of cache misses for either an n-point FFT or the sorting of n numbers is \\Theta(1+(n/L)(1+\\logZ n)). We also give an \\Theta(mnp)-work algorithm to multiply an m\\times n matrix by an n\\times p matrix that incurs \\Theta(1 + (mn+np+mp)/L + mnp/L\\sqrt{Z}) cache faults.\nL16: We introduce an ``ideal-cache'' model to analyze our algorithms, and we prove that an optimal cache-oblivious algorithm designed for two levels of memory is also optimal for multiple levels. We also prove that any optimal cache-oblivious algorithm is also optimal in the previously studied HMM and SUMH models. Algorithms developed for these earlier models are perforce cache-aware: their behavior varies as a function of hardware-dependent parameters which must be tuned to attain optimality.\nL17: Our cache-oblivious algorithms achieve the same asymptotic optimality, but without any tuning.\nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: This page maintained by: Matteo Frigo (athena@fftw.org). Last updated: Mon Sep 16 16:14:09 2019\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://fftw.org/fftw-paper-ieee.pdf)\nciteturn11view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 16; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://fftw.org/fftw-paper-ieee.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1776\nL0@P0: 1\nL1@P0: The Design and Implementation of FFTW3\nL2@P0: Matteo Frigo and Steven G. Johnson\nL3@P0: (Invited Paper)\nL4@P0: Abstract— FFTW is an implementation of the discrete Fourier\nL5@P0: transform (DFT) that adapts to the hardware in order to\nL6@P0: maximize performance. This paper shows that such an approach\nL7@P0: can yield an implementation that is competitive with hand\u0002optimized libraries, and describes the software structure that\nL8@P0: makes our current FFTW3 version flexible and adaptive. We\nL9@P0: further discuss a new algorithm for real-data DFTs of prime size,\nL10@P0: a new way of implementing DFTs by means of machine-specific\nL11@P0: “SIMD” instructions, and how a special-purpose compiler can\nL12@P0: derive optimized implementations of the discrete cosine and sine\nL13@P0: transforms automatically from a DFT algorithm.\nL14@P0: Index Terms— FFT, adaptive software, Fourier transform,\nL15@P0: cosine transform, Hartley transform, I/O tensor.\nL16@P0: I. INTRODUCTION\nL17@P0: F\nL18@P0: FTW [1] is a widely used free-software library that\nL19@P0: computes the discrete Fourier transform (DFT) and its\nL20@P0: various special cases. Its performance is competitive even with\nL21@P0: vendor-optimized programs, but unlike these programs, FFTW\nL22@P0: is not tuned to a fixed machine. Instead, FFTW uses a planner\nL23@P0: to adapt its algorithms to the hardware in order to maximize\nL24@P0: performance. The input to the planner is a problem, a multi\u0002dimensional loop of multi-dimensional DFTs. The planner\nL25@P0: applies a set of rules to recursively decompose a problem into\nL26@P0: simpler sub-problems of the same type. “Sufficiently simple”\nL27@P0: problems are solved directly by optimized, straight-line code\nL28@P0: that is automatically generated by a special-purpose compiler.\nL29@P0: This paper describes the overall structure of FFTW as well as\nL30@P0: the specific improvements in FFTW3, our latest version.\nL31@P0: FFTW is fast, but its speed does not come at the expense of\nL32@P0: flexibility. In fact, FFTW is probably the most flexible DFT\nL33@P0: library available:\nL34@P0: • FFTW is written in portable C and runs well on many\nL35@P0: architectures and operating systems.\nL36@P0: • FFTW computes DFTs in O(n log n) time for any\nL37@P0: length n. (Most other DFT implementations are either\nL38@P0: restricted to a subset of sizes or they become Θ(n\nL39@P0: 2\nL40@P0: ) for\nL41@P0: certain values of n, for example when n is prime.)\nL42@P0: • FFTW imposes no restrictions on the rank (dimension\u0002ality) of multi-dimensional transforms. (Most other im\u0002plementations are limited to one-dimensional, or at most\nL43@P0: two- and three-dimensional data.)\nL44@P0: • FFTW supports multiple and/or strided DFTs; for exam\u0002ple, to transform a 3-component vector field or a portion\nL45@P0: M. Frigo is with the IBM Austin Research Laboratory, 11501 Burnet\nL46@P0: Road, Austin, TX 78758. He was supported in part by the Defense Advanced\nL47@P0: Research Projects Agency (DARPA) under contract No. NBCH30390004.\nL48@P0: S. G. Johnson is with the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 77\nL49@P0: Mass. Ave. Rm. 2-388, Cambridge, MA 02139. He was supported in part\nL50@P0: by the Materials Research Science and Engineering Center program of the\nL51@P0: National Science Foundation under award DMR-9400334.\nL52@P0: of a multi-dimensional array. (Most implementations sup\u0002port only a single DFT of contiguous data.)\nL53@P0: • FFTW supports DFTs of real data, as well as of real\nL54@P0: symmetric/antisymmetric data (also called discrete co\u0002sine/sine transforms).\nL55@P0: The interaction of the user with FFTW occurs in two\nL56@P0: stages: planning, in which FFTW adapts to the hardware,\nL57@P0: and execution, in which FFTW performs useful work for the\nL58@P0: user. To compute a DFT, the user first invokes the FFTW\nL59@P0: planner, specifying the problem to be solved. The problem is\nL60@P0: a data structure that describes the “shape” of the input data—\nL61@P0: array sizes and memory layouts—but does not contain the data\nL62@P0: itself. In return, the planner yields a plan, an executable data\nL63@P0: structure that accepts the input data and computes the desired\nL64@P0: DFT. Afterwards, the user can execute the plan as many times\nL65@P0: as desired.\nL66@P0: The FFTW planner works by measuring the actual run time\nL67@P0: of many different plans and by selecting the fastest one. This\nL68@P0: process is analogous to what a programmer would do by hand\nL69@P0: when tuning a program to a fixed machine, but in FFTW’s case\nL70@P0: no manual intervention is required. Because of the repeated\nL71@P0: performance measurements, however, the planner tends to be\nL72@P0: time-consuming. In performance-critical applications, many\nL73@P0: transforms of the same size are typically required, and there\u0002fore a large one-time cost is usually acceptable. Otherwise,\nL74@P0: FFTW provides a mode of operation where the planner quickly\nL75@P0: returns a “reasonable” plan that is not necessarily the fastest.\nL76@P0: The planner generates plans according to rules that recur\u0002sively decompose a problem into simpler sub-problems. When\nL77@P0: the problem becomes “sufficiently simple,” FFTW produces\nL78@P0: a plan that calls a fragment of optimized straight-line code\nL79@P0: that solves the problem directly. These fragments are called\nL80@P0: codelets in FFTW’s lingo. You can envision a codelet as\nL81@P0: computing a “small” DFT, but many variations and special\nL82@P0: cases exist. For example, a codelet might be specialized to\nL83@P0: compute the DFT of real input (as opposed to complex).\nL84@P0: FFTW’s speed depends therefore on two factors. First, the\nL85@P0: decomposition rules must produce a space of plans that is rich\nL86@P0: enough to contain “good” plans for most machines. Second,\nL87@P0: the codelets must be fast, since they ultimately perform all the\nL88@P0: real work.\nL89@P0: FFTW’s codelets are generated automatically by a special\u0002purpose compiler called genfft. Most users do not interact\nL90@P0: with genfft at all: the standard FFTW distribution contains\nL91@P0: a set of about 150 pre-generated codelets that cover the most\nL92@P0: common uses. Users with special needs can use genfft\nL93@P0: to generate their own codelets. genfft is useful because\nL94@P0: of the following features. From a high-level mathematical\nL95@P0: description of a DFT algorithm, genfft derives an optimized\nL96@P0-1: implementation automatically. From a complex-DFT algo-Published in Proc. IEEE, vol. 93, no. 2, pp. 216–231 (2005).\nL97@P1: rithm, genfft automatically derives an optimized algorithm\nL98@P1: for the real-input DFT. We take advantage of this property to\nL99@P1: implement real-data DFTs (Section VII), as well as to exploit\nL100@P1: machine-specific “SIMD” instructions (Section IX). Similarly,\nL101@P1: genfft automatically derives codelets for the discrete cosine\nL102@P1: (DCT) and sine (DST) transforms (Section VIII). We summa\u0002rize genfft in Section VI, while a full description appears\nL103@P1: in [2].\nL104@P1: We have produced three major implementations of FFTW,\nL105@P1: each building on the experience of the previous system.\nL106@P1: FFTW1 [3] (1997) introduced the idea of generating codelets\nL107@P1: automatically, and of letting a planner search for the best\nL108@P1: combination of codelets. FFTW2 (1998) incorporated a new\nL109@P1: version of genfft [2]. genfft did not change much in\nL110@P1: FFTW3 (2003), but the runtime structure was completely\nL111@P1: rewritten to allow for a much larger space of plans. This paper\nL112@P1: describes the main ideas common to all FFTW systems, the\nL113@P1: runtime structure of FFTW3, and the modifications to genfft\nL114@P1: since FFTW2.\nL115@P1: Previous work on adaptive systems includes [3]–[11]. In\nL116@P1: particular, SPIRAL [9], [10] is another system focused on\nL117@P1: optimization of Fourier transforms and related algorithms,\nL118@P1: but it has distinct differences from FFTW. SPIRAL searches\nL119@P1: at compile-time over a space of mathematically equivalent\nL120@P1: formulas expressed in a “tensor-product” language, whereas\nL121@P1: FFTW searches at runtime over the formalism discussed in\nL122@P1: Section IV, which explicitly includes low-level details, such as\nL123@P1: strides and memory alignments, that are not as easily expressed\nL124@P1: using tensor products. SPIRAL generates machine-dependent\nL125@P1: code, whereas FFTW’s codelets are machine-independent.\nL126@P1: FFTW’s search uses dynamic programming [12, chapter 16],\nL127@P1: while the SPIRAL project has experimented with a wider\nL128@P1: range of search strategies including machine-learning tech\u0002niques [13].\nL129@P1: The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. We\nL130@P1: begin with a general overview of fast Fourier transforms in\nL131@P1: Section II. Then, in Section III, we compare the performance\nL132@P1: of FFTW and other DFT implementations. Section IV de\u0002scribes the space of plans explored by FFTW and how the\nL133@P1: FFTW planner works. Section V describes our experiences\nL134@P1: in the practical usage of FFTW. Section VI summarizes how\nL135@P1: genfft works. Section VII explains how FFTW computes\nL136@P1: DFTs of real data. Section VIII describes how genfft\nL137@P1: generates DCT and DST codelets, as well as how FFTW\nL138@P1: handles these transforms in the general case. Section IX tells\nL139@P1: how FFTW exploits SIMD instructions.\nL140@P1: II. FFT OVERVIEW\nL141@P1: The (forward, one-dimensional) discrete Fourier transform\nL142@P1: of an array X of n complex numbers is the array Y given by\nL143@P1: Y [k] =\nL144@P1: nX−1\nL145@P1: j=0\nL146@P1: X[j]ω\nL147@P1: jk\nL148@P1: n\nL149@P1: , (1)\nL150@P1: where 0 ≤ k < n and ωn = exp(−2π\nL151@P1: √\nL152@P1: −1/n). Implemented\nL153@P1: directly, Eq. (1) would require Θ(n\nL154@P1: 2\nL155@P1: ) operations; fast Fourier\nL156@P1: transforms are O(n log n) algorithms to compute the same\nL157@P1: result. The most important FFT (and the one primarily used in\nL158@P1: FFTW) is known as the “Cooley-Tukey” algorithm, after the\nL159@P1: two authors who rediscovered and popularized it in 1965 [14],\nL160@P1: although it had been previously known as early as 1805 by\nL161@P1: Gauss as well as by later re-inventors [15]. The basic idea\nL162@P1: behind this FFT is that a DFT of a composite size n = n1n2\nL163@P1: can be re-expressed in terms of smaller DFTs of sizes n1 and\nL164@P1: n2—essentially, as a two-dimensional DFT of size n1 × n2\nL165@P1: where the output is transposed. The choices of factorizations\nL166@P1: of n, combined with the many different ways to implement the\nL167@P1: data re-orderings of the transpositions, have led to numerous\nL168@P1: implementation strategies for the Cooley-Tukey FFT, with\nL169@P1: many variants distinguished by their own names [16], [17].\nL170@P1: FFTW implements a space of many such variants, as described\nL171@P1: later, but here we derive the basic algorithm, identify its key\nL172@P1: features, and outline some important historical variations and\nL173@P1: their relation to FFTW.\nL174@P1: The Cooley-Tukey algorithm can be derived as follows. If\nL175@P1: n can be factored into n = n1n2, Eq. (1) can be rewritten by\n"
